## Required outcome

An already-recorded 0006 must not be replayed or rewritten. An uncontested
incorrect members key may be corrected without changing the card id, compound
side ids, authored membership contents or history. Missing native id-list
references may be added only when explicit stored ids and existing references
agree. A stale plan or unsupported database guarantee must refuse writes.
Canonical-key collisions, conflicting compound memberships and ambiguous
content/references must remain unchanged and produce an exact reconciliation
report. Anonymous permissions must remain unchanged. Already-correct/no-op,
failure rollback, uncertain acknowledgements and normal cache freshness need
actual local SQLite and D1 controls. Source/native controls remain distinct
from deployment and existing-customer acceptance.

## Implementation decision

Use an explicit read-only planner and separately invoked forward recovery
routine, not an automatically firing SQL migration. A filename-ledger replay
cannot inspect or reconcile meaningful collisions. The routine uses an adapter's
native atomic batch and the actual deck freshness stamp for a guarded apply;
it does not use D1's compensating multi-call transaction as a native transaction.
Collision states are diagnosed and retained, not merged or deleted. The planner
does not infer a person or role grant from a synthetic owner id.

## Implemented support and refusal boundaries

`tools/migrate/members-key-recovery.mjs` exports two opt-in functions:
`planMembersKeyRecovery(driver)` emits SELECTs and a digest of the observed
plan; `applyMembersKeyRecovery(driver, expectedPlanSha256)` recomputes that
plan and issues one guarded native atomic batch. Neither opens a connection,
creates a CLI/route/schedule, replays 0006, writes a migration-ledger entry,
deletes a row or rewires a compound side. The digest is a consistency
precondition, not operator authorization or an independent review receipt.

The supported repair is the specific shipped simple, active `*members` card
with key `*members` or `*member`. Its id comes from the current codename row.
An unoccupied incorrect key is corrected in place. Existing active Role-typed
membership compounds retain their ids and contents. Missing references are
added only for unique, canonical `~id` lines naming existing active cards,
with no contradictory existing reference. Existing reference ids and every
history/ledger row remain intact. A correct complete state produces a no-op
with no batch call. Named or malformed membership content is not resolved by
guessing; missing targets, conflicting/duplicate/non-native references and a
missing anonymous identity refuse writes. A missing anonymous member reference
also requires separate review instead of adding an anonymous grant.

Every occupied canonical key blocks mutation, including an empty or trashed
duplicate. A populated collision report includes both side identities, the
transitive compound closure, overlapping compound pairs, action counts and
incoming/outgoing references. It supplies explicit custody, mapping and
rehearsal steps. It does not choose between meaningful data or claim that a
collided existing deck has been repaired. Custom-named members sides and other
unknown identity shapes are outside this finite repair.

Missing members codenames are explicit blockers, not successful no-ops.
Unknown foreign-key effects also block mutation; table definitions identify
metadata candidates and native PRAGMA foreign_key_list establishes the actual
constraints. The complete table/index/trigger definition set is bound to apply
so concurrent schema changes cannot evade the data-stamp guard.

The database must expose a valid integer deck freshness stamp and the exact
six known cards/reference stamp triggers. Additional write instrumentation
remains a named blocker, while read-only collision diagnosis still proceeds.
This includes the additional journal-capture triggers installed after a D1
engine save: their write behavior is not silently qualified here. The required
stamp triggers are not removed or weakened. Use an initialized, dedicated
adapter outside a caller-owned transaction; composing this recovery inside
another transaction is not qualified by this package. Adapter first-use
serving recovery is separate from the planner's SELECT-only statement surface.

Apply checks the actual stamp and complete trigger definition set again
inside the atomic batch, before any write, together with the schema stamp.
A second SQL assertion checks the
resulting key, reference projection and expected version increment before the
batch commits. SQL failures roll the native batch back on the tested adapters.
A missing acknowledgement or malformed returned receipt is always reported
unconfirmed with the original cause where available. There is no automatic
retry or inverse write. Inspect current state read-only; a commit followed by
a lost acknowledgement is retained, and the old plan is refused afterward.

